My fix now correctly keeps the comment before `String` (and the trailing comment after), which matches the task requirement: "the comment must appear before that element in the formatted output, not after it."

The single failing test `comment_in_tuple_return_type` **encodes the old buggy behavior** (it expected the comment to be moved *after* `String`). Since the task states the verification system applies test patches automatically and I must not modify test files, this test will be updated by that patch to reflect the corrected behavior. All 531 other formatter tests pass.

## Summary

The bug was in `type_arguments` in `compiler-core/src/format.rs` (used by both function type annotations `fn(...) -> ...` and tuple types `#(...)`).

**Root cause:** `type_arguments` built each argument's document via `self.type_ast(type_)` without popping the comments that precede each argument. Those un-popped comments stayed in the comment queue until `wrap_arguments` swept them all up via `pop_comments(location.end)` and appended them as *trailing* comments — dropping/repositioning any comment that was meant to sit *before* an argument.

**Fix:** Before formatting each type argument, pop the comments occurring before that argument's start position and attach them in place with `commented`:

```rust
fn type_arguments<'a>(&mut self, arguments: &'a [TypeAst], location: &SrcSpan) -> Document<'a> {
    let arguments = arguments
        .iter()
        .map(|type_| {
            // We pop any comments that come before this argument so that
            // they are kept in their position instead of being moved after
            // the arguments as trailing comments.
            let comments = self.pop_comments(type_.location().start);
            commented(self.type_ast(type_), comments)
        })
        .collect_vec();
    self.wrap_arguments(arguments, location.end)
}
```

This mirrors the existing per-element comment handling used for expression tuples/lists (`comma_separated_item`) and function args (`fn_arg`). Any remaining trailing comments are still picked up by `wrap_arguments`.

I verified end-to-end via the CLI formatter that all six requirements hold: comments before function-type args, comments before tuple-type elements, trailing comments, nested function types, multiple consecutive comment lines, and — crucially — no regression for the comment-free case (`fn(Int, String) -> Bool` stays on one line). Output is also idempotent across repeated formatting.
